          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bob claims that when he buys a lottery ticket, he will either win or lose the lottery, so he has a 50% chance of winning. Explain what is wrong with this logic.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Winning and losing are not equally likely. Two possible outcomes do not imply a 50% chance of each.",
      "worked": "The sample space can be written as $S=\\{\\text{win},\\text{lose}\\}$. The rule $P(E)=\\dfrac{|E|}{|S|}$ applies only when the outcomes are equally likely. Bob has not established that winning and losing are equally likely, so he cannot conclude that the chance of winning is 50%.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
  },
